        "id": "plain-divinity-choice-unfair",
        "item": "choice-unfair",
        "when": "created",
        "title": "The wrong is taking what another is owed",
        "text": "Eating and enjoying comfort can be good. Taking the meal a neighbour needs is wrong because it puts personal comfort above what the neighbour is owed. The fault is in the choice, not in food itself.",
        "presentation": "explanation"
      },
      {
        "id": "plain-divinity-harm-not-guilt",
        "item": "harm-not-guilt",
        "when": "created",
        "title": "Being hurt is not the same as doing wrong",
        "text": "A wound is harm suffered; stealing the meal is a wrong deliberately chosen. Comparing them as losses must not turn suffering into evidence of guilt.",
        "presentation": "explanation"
      },
      {
        "id": "plain-divinity-augustine-loss",
        "item": "augustine-loss",
        "when": "created",
        "title": "Augustine: evil is good damaged or missing, not separate stuff",
        "text": "Augustine thinks evil is damage to, or loss of, something good rather than a separate evil material. A wound illustrates the idea: the body remains, but health is missing. His belief in a good creation is a starting point, not something the example proves.",
        "presentation": "explanation"
      },
      {
        "id": "plain-divinity-aided-choice",
        "item": "aided-choice",
        "when": "created",
        "title": "Augustine: God helps the person want and act",
        "text": "Augustine says God can help a person want and do what is right. The person still chooses and acts; help does not make the human actor disappear.",
        "presentation": "explanation"
      },
      {
        "id": "plain-divinity-condition-not-act",
        "item": "condition-not-act",
