      },
      {
        "id": "plain-divinity-trinity-bounded",
        "item": "trinity-bounded",
        "when": "created",
        "title": "Trinity: one God, real distinctions—explained here, not proved",
        "text": "The doctrine of the Trinity names Father, Son and Holy Spirit as one God. They are not separate gods or one person taking turns in different roles. Aquinas says observation of created things cannot prove this doctrine.",
        "presentation": "explanation"
      },
      {
        "id": "plain-divinity-ritual-not-cosmology",
        "item": "ritual-not-cosmology",
        "when": "created",
        "title": "Using bodies in a rite does not prove belief in one good creator",
        "text": "In the Lyon ritual, ministers lay hands and a Gospel book on a believer while promising forgiveness and God’s Spirit. Using physical objects does not tell us whether this community believed in one good creator or opposed sources of good and evil. The Book of Two Principles argues for opposed sources; the ritual does not state that account.",
