        "item": "renounce-not-abandon",
        "when": "created",
        "title": "Rejecting worldly desires does not cancel the duty to love enemies",
        "text": "The candidate promises to reject worldly desires and to love enemies. Giving up those desires does not remove duties toward other people.",
        "presentation": "explanation"
      },
      {
        "id": "plain-divinity-instrumental-baptism",
        "item": "instrumental-baptism",
        "when": "created",
        "title": "Aquinas: in baptism, God works through water to give inward grace",
        "text": "For Aquinas, baptism washes the body while God works through the water to give grace: a new life within the soul. The water is a tool, not a second god. This depends on his belief that God chose baptism to give that gift.",
        "presentation": "explanation"
      },
      {
        "id": "plain-divinity-rites-different",
        "item": "rites-different",
        "when": "created",
        "title": "Both rites use bodies; they disagree about what God appointed",
        "text": "The Lyon rite uses hands and a Gospel book; Aquinas discusses baptism with water. Both claim an inward gift through physical actions, but disagree about the authorized rite and how it gives that gift.",
